"""Assemble the completed evaluation report from frozen results and adjudications."""
from pathlib import Path
import json
R=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]
def read(n):return json.loads((R/'analysis'/n).read_text())
def p(t):return {'type':'paragraph','text':t}
def h(t):return {'type':'heading','text':t}
def table(headers,rows,widths,note=None):return {'type':'table','headers':headers,'rows':rows,'widths':widths,'font_size':9.5,'note':note}
br={'type':'page_break'}
recon=read('reconciliation.json')
blocks=[h('Assessment'),p('The prospective comparison is complete: three source cases, two frozen coding specifications, three independent coders per condition, masked proposition alignment, two independent source audits, and coordinator reconciliation. All eighteen coding outputs survived the usage interruption. The reviewers resumed their saved contexts; no coding or alignment run was repeated.'),p('Version 0.4 did not demonstrate better overall independent repeatability or fewer source-supported evidence-basis errors in this sample. On the same 42 matched propositions, the three coders agreed on all six common fields in 24 cases under version 0.3 and 21 under version 0.4. Agreement improved modestly for deontic force, remained unchanged for grammar, and declined for institutional type, governance level, and the Boolean dispute flag.'),p('Source reconciliation identifies five evidence-basis error groups: one under version 0.3 and four under version 0.4. It identifies no interpretation-changing error or material extraction/qualifier omission in either condition. Those are bounded review findings, not proof of error-free coding. The richer version 0.4 schema exposes more identity and support claims to inspection, so the counts do not establish that it has a higher underlying error rate.'),p('I would retain version 0.4 as the working representation because its separate support dimensions make claims easier to inspect. I would retain independent overlap and source-based reconciliation as part of the analytical method. The paper can demonstrate an auditable, review-assisted institutional reconstruction; this test does not support a claim that version 0.4 independently classifies every field reliably or outperforms version 0.3.'),h('Cases and bounded outcomes'),table(['Case','Source','Conclusion supported by the retained record'],[
 ['F01','PR #26586: previous-release tests','The contribution adds v24.0.1 and merges. Participants debate release-selection approaches; the merge does not establish adoption of one general retention policy.'],
 ['F02','PR #14441: wallet backport','The review records specific edit suggestions, an author update report, resulting release-note content, and integration. Some individual reply targets and correction history remain uncertain.'],
 ['F03','PR #7819: opt-in-RBF checkbox','Participants distinguish reviewing a proposal from exposing it before a replacement workflow exists. Both this PR and its referenced successor close unmerged.']
 ],[.55,1.95,4.6], 'Exact source anchors and limits for eight usable case conclusions appear in analysis/adoptable_case_claims.json. These conclusions do not replace the original coding records.'),br,
 h('Primary source-review findings'),p('Both reviewers examined all 18 annotations, 587 retained units, 327 relations, and 83 source entries, including unanimous claims. Reviewer A submitted 50 bounded findings; reviewer B submitted 12. These totals include alternatives and convention questions, so they are not error counts. Reconciliation maps every finding into 59 distinct issue groups: five primary errors, nineteen convention deviations, ten criterion ambiguities, twenty-four defensible alternatives, and one unresolved referent.'),table(['Case','0.3 evidence-basis groups','0.4 evidence-basis groups','Affected run–group incidences: 0.3 / 0.4'],[
 ['F01','0','4','0 / 5'],['F02','0','0','0 / 0'],['F03','1','0','2 / 0'],['Total','1','4','2 / 5']
 ],[.6,1.8,1.8,2.9],'Groups identify distinct proposition or relationship issues within a case. A shared issue can affect several runs; those incidences are not independent errors. No interpretation-changing or material coverage-loss group was confirmed.'),table(['Group / condition','Issue','Required narrowing or correction'],[
 ['P01 / 0.4','Later pruning in a follow-up receives the containing PR’s identifier.','Leave the future proposal unidentified; keep the current PR only as the discussion episode.'],
 ['P02 / 0.4','A separate cleanup proposal and its general endorsement also receive the containing PR’s identifier.','Separate the future contribution from the present contribution and from general-cleanup assent.'],
 ['P03 / 0.4','A repeated open-PR method receives a full-proposition target although it omits the original stopping condition.','Retain correspondence to the broader method; do not claim repetition of the complete conditional proposition.'],
 ['P04 / 0.4','One uptake relation reverses initiating proposal and response.','Reverse its direction, or retain the direction with an appropriate clarification relation.'],
 ['P05 / 0.3','Two runs call retained body wording earlier than a comment without sentence-level edit history.','Retain semantic correspondence while withholding the specific wording chronology.']
 ],[1.1,2.65,3.35]),p('The coordinator treats P03 and P04 as evidence-basis problems because their stored links overstate full-proposition correspondence or reverse a defined relationship. The reviewers differed on whether these effects were substantive evidence errors or convention issues. Treating both as convention-only would reduce the version 0.4 count from four to two; treating an additional under-labeled action basis as an evidence error would increase it to five. Neither sensitivity treatment shows fewer evidence-basis groups under version 0.4. These small descriptive counts still cannot rank population performance.'),p('P05 requires care: a later PR updated_at value does not prove that a particular body sentence changed. The problem is the unsupported earlier/later wording assertion. The source supports neither certainty about its original wording date nor certainty that the reverse order occurred.'),br,
 h('What should remain unresolved'),p('The most consequential unresolved distinction concerns recognition. In F01, a participant quotes the proposed minimum of recent non-EOL releases and says, “I tend to agree.” The record supports tentative assent to that quoted floor. It does not uniquely determine whether the assent treats it as an institutional standard, nor whether it endorses every qualification of the original formulation. Both reviewers preserve ordinary-assent and local-expectation readings. No count of agreeing coders can establish a binding or generally adopted policy from that passage.'),p('F02 illustrates a different boundary. The integrated artifact contains the requested plural identifier, but the packet does not locate the original typo and its correction operation. Observing a resulting state and observing a transformation are different claims. The guide permits an observed act or artifact, so the bounded alternatives do not justify retrospectively imposing a missing before/after-history requirement.'),p('Other differences concern the analytical layer: a request to perform an operational action can also advance a proposal about a recurring testing arrangement; an articulated technical method can remain a strategy candidate or an engineering suggestion; a question about future scope can leave a historical work report uncontested. The ledger preserves these alternatives rather than manufacturing a uniquely correct label.'),p('The one unresolved referent concerns what the bot would ignore after a reaction: the review, comment, or listing. The likely interpretation can remain explicit as an inference. The source does not establish actual bot execution. A narrower downstream statement can describe the announced conditional response without choosing its software implementation referent.'),h('Corrections and conventions'),p('Most convention deviations concern parse_mode: coders sometimes mark a parse pragmatic even though the selected action is explicit and only recommendation force is inferred. Other deviations concern replacement-word presentation, repeated-quotation speech acts, a preferred purpose/condition representation, and the scope of the uptake relation label. These issues matter for comparable representations, but they do not automatically change the institutional interpretation.'),p('Reconciliation does not automatically accept a reviewer’s preferred form. For example, a consistently inferred avoid-rushing predicate can preserve the same meaning as the literal not-rush predicate. The ledger retains that alternative. Likewise, a repeated quotation’s label should not simply copy a potentially debatable original label; the source-based speech-act convention must govern both.'),p('The complete ledger retains the original reviewer diagnosis, the coordinator’s grouping and rationale, affected annotation/run IDs, exact evidence, and the required downstream limitation. Original annotations, independent agreement, and tested criteria remain unchanged. The eight bounded case conclusions use these limitations; the archive does not claim to contain a negotiated consensus annotation.'),br]
blocks += read('report_agreement.json')
blocks += [h('What the sample does not test'),p('The 314 statement occurrences receive 297 operational and 17 collective-choice target assignments; none receives a constitutional or epistemic target assignment. Observed-action assignments are likewise confined to those first two levels. These are repeated coding occurrences, not independent events. The cases therefore provide no validation of discrimination at constitutional or epistemic levels, or of analysis of later value-driven forks. No such extension was added to this evaluation.')]
blocks += read('report_operations.json')
blocks += [h('Readiness for the methodology working paper'),p('The completed record supports a bounded methods demonstration with transparent source selection, independent coding, explicit uncertainty, and a documented review stage. It does not require a new human-coded reference set merely to make that claim. It also does not support claims of human-equivalent accuracy, cross-model replication, automated policy choice, or measured time and cost savings.'),p('For downstream use, apply the five primary corrections or claim restrictions, preserve the unresolved recognition and application boundaries, and avoid presenting one reconciled label as if all coders originally selected it. The current frozen annotations remain research outputs with known defects; the corrected case conclusions and adjudication ledger identify what can be used without those overclaims.'),p('The next release should repair the packet/checker metadata mismatch and clarify only distinctions needed for the intended conclusions: proposal identity versus discussion identity, partial response versus full-proposition restatement, resulting artifacts versus observed transformations, and the same-actor profile convention. If those revisions support a claim of improved independent performance, they need a new prospective test. This completed comparison should remain the record of what version 0.4 actually achieved.')]
human=read('human_coding_note.json')
extra=' Alpha of 0.80 is not the same as 80% raw agreement. For this structured codebook task, report a measure for each commensurable field rather than one pooled score across unlike judgments.'
if extra not in human[1]['text']:human[1]['text'] += extra
human[-1]['text']='This run completed independent source review followed by coordinator reconciliation. It did not measure negotiated agreement among the original coders and did not compare AI with human performance. A future agent discussion can preserve initial outputs, record each evidence-based revision or retained disagreement, and report final negotiated decisions separately. Agreement after discussion cannot retroactively improve the independent reliability statistic.'
(R/'analysis/human_coding_note.json').write_text(json.dumps(human,indent=2,ensure_ascii=False)+'\n')
blocks += [br]+human+[h('Why a working paper remains defensible'),p('A defensible baseline can show what the method extracts, where independently generated interpretations converge, which decisions require reconciliation, and how sources constrain the final claims. Perfect independent agreement is unnecessary. Low or ambiguous agreement on institutional type or governance level should limit claims about those fields, rather than disappear behind a final consensus. Human judgment still determines research priorities and value tradeoffs; the analytical coding and source checking can remain AI-assisted and inspectable.'),br]
methods=read('report_methods.json')
for b in methods:
 if b['type']=='paragraph':
  b['text']=b['text'].replace('A separate alignment context receives','A separate alignment context received').replace('Two fresh source-review contexts receive','Two fresh source-review contexts received').replace('The coordinator reconciles','The coordinator reconciled')
blocks += methods
blocks += [h('Interruption and continuity'),p('A weekly usage interruption stopped the source reviewers after coding and alignment had completed. A recovery check reverified every frozen run input and output and the alignment mapping. Both existing reviewer contexts resumed from their saved work under the same isolation restrictions. No aggregate agreement, mask key, or other reviewer’s findings entered either review context. The archive retains the interruption checkpoint, resumption log, first-complete reviews, correction histories, and final freezes. The interface did not expose credit accounting, so no itemized credit explanation or efficiency estimate is claimed.'),h('Replication files'),p('The accompanying evidence archive contains frozen source packets, selected canonical corpus lines, selection and exposure inventories, both specifications and checkers, all eighteen first/final annotations and repair records, alignment and review inputs/outputs, all derived metrics, the reconciliation ledger, bounded case conclusions, and verification scripts. PACKAGE_MANIFEST.json and tools/verify_package.py support byte-integrity checks. The full 25,508-record parent corpus is not duplicated; its SHA-256 identity and the full-selection verification result are retained.'),p('The original preparation and assembly scripts retain their historical workspace assumptions. Do not rerun masking or input assembly over the completed review directories. Run reproduction commands in a copy, because metric and check scripts write their results. README.txt lists the portable commands and external-corpus requirement.'),h('Methodological references')]
refs=[
 'Hayes, A. F., and K. Krippendorff. 2007. Answering the Call for a Standard Reliability Measure for Coding Data. Communication Methods and Measures 1(1):77–89. https://doi.org/10.1080/19312450709336664',
 'Campbell, J. L., C. Quincy, J. Osserman, and O. K. Pedersen. 2013. Coding In-depth Semistructured Interviews: Problems of Unitization and Intercoder Reliability and Agreement. Sociological Methods & Research 42(3):294–320. https://doi.org/10.1177/0049124113500475',
 'O’Connor, C., and H. Joffe. 2020. Intercoder Reliability in Qualitative Research: Debates and Practical Guidelines. International Journal of Qualitative Methods 19:1–13. https://doi.org/10.1177/1609406919899220',
 'Krippendorff, K. 2016–2017. Communication Scholars Oral History Project; interview by Jefferson Pooley; transcript substantially edited by Krippendorff, p. 52. University of Pennsylvania, Annenberg School for Communication. Source URL and complete metadata appear in analysis/methodological_references.json.'
]
blocks += [p(x) for x in refs]
out={'title':'IG2 coding evaluation: specifications 0.3 and 0.4','subtitle':'Prospective comparison | November 7, 2026\nIndependent coding, source review, and reconciliation complete','blocks':blocks}
(R/'analysis/report_content.json').write_text(json.dumps(out,indent=2,ensure_ascii=False)+'\n')
status={'kind':'completed_prospective_evaluation','final_validation_report':True,'source_audits_complete':True,'independent_coding_complete':True,'agreement_complete':True,'reconciliation_complete':True,'original_annotations_repaired':False,'original_coder_negotiation_performed':False,'overall_v04_improvement_demonstrated':False,'cross_model_or_human_performance_test':False,'bounded_case_claims':8,'primary_error_groups':5,'manuscripts_edited':False}
(R/'analysis/report_status.json').write_text(json.dumps(status,indent=2)+'\n')
print('Report blocks:',len(blocks),'prose words:',sum(len(b.get('text','').split()) for b in blocks))
